\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: structural degeneration generalizes.}
Two seeds reach stable convergence with $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$; s43 oscillates ($\text{PR}{\approx}0.55$, $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.12$).
Hacking is more severe than FOLIO: converged seeds show 0--1.4\% genuine (s42: 100\% TS, s44: 98.6\% CE) versus ${\sim}$8\% non-hacking on FOLIO; ProofFOL's simpler predicates lower the shortcut threshold.
Notably, s42 exhibits 100\% TS---absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds---showing hacking-mode repertoire is seed- and dataset-specific (1/3 seeds, not systematic).
All seeds have $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$---far below the pre-training baseline ($0.31$)---consistent with the LOO tautological attractor being metric-intrinsic.

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability.}
ISR bonus reduces stable convergence from 2/3 to 0/3 seeds---superficially, another optimization failure.
Hacking-mode classification reverses this reading: ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% for exec-only (permutation $p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$), with s42 and s44 achieving 100\% genuine despite training collapse (Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}).
The models that failed to converge produced structurally honest proofs.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
Eval-time ISR confirms the asymmetry: on ProofFOL, hacking constructions yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$ while genuine proofs yield $\text{ISR}{=}0.27$ (s42) and $0.20$ (s44)---ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts.
On FOLIO (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0.50$), $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$, so ISR erroneously rewards tautological hacking (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL (baseline $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.31$), hacking does not satisfy ISR, so the bonus correctly suppresses shortcuts at the cost of convergence stability.
The same metric produces opposite outcomes---Goodhart collapse where hacking satisfies it (FOLIO), beneficial instability where it does not (ProofFOL)---determined by the dataset's hacking geometry.

